Added `__traits(vtblSymbol)` and `__traits(interfaceSymbol)`, and `__traits(initSymbol)` now yields a symbol slice

`__traits(vtblSymbol, C)` is the vtable of class `C`.

`__traits(interfaceSymbol, C)` is the `Interface[]` array of class or interface
`C`, the one `TypeInfo_Class.interfaces` refers to.

Like `__traits(initSymbol)`, they refer to symbols whose address is only known at
link time. All three now yield a symbol slice: an instance of the internal
`object.__SymbolSlice!T` struct, which holds the length and the address of the
immutable symbol and converts to `immutable(T)[]`. During
CTFE its `length` and `ptr` can be used and it can be stored, for example in
static data built at compile time; only the contents cannot be read. Its fields
are `@system`, so `@safe` code can read `length` and `ptr` but not modify them.
Use `typeof` to name its type:

---
struct S { int a = 1; long b = 2; }
class C { void f() {} }

struct Info
{
    typeof(__traits(initSymbol, S)) init;
    typeof(__traits(vtblSymbol, C)) vtbl;
}

immutable Info info = Info(__traits(initSymbol, S), __traits(vtblSymbol, C));
static assert(info.init.length == S.sizeof);
---

The class passed to `__traits(vtblSymbol)` or `__traits(interfaceSymbol)` must have
completed semantic analysis, so they cannot be used inside the class itself, outside
its member function bodies.

Previously `__traits(initSymbol)` returned a `const(void)[]` and could not be used
during CTFE at all. Code that checks its type with
`is(typeof(...) == const(void)[])` needs updating, and a custom `object.d` must
define `__SymbolSlice` to support these traits.
